{
 "id": "Matrices-Multiplication-Properties",
 "title_en": "Properties of matrix multiplication",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "We have already seen that matrix multiplication is not in general commutative, i.e., $AB$ and $BA$ may be different, even if they are both defined. Sometimes it can happen that $AB=BA$ for specific matrices $A$ and $B$. In this case, we say that $A$ and $B$ **commute**.",
    "sha": "343cb9be97d7",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "The following are some properties of matrix multiplication. Notice that these properties hold only when the size of matrices are such that the products are defined.",
    "sha": "d2e07cf38221",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "proposition",
   "title": {
    "en": "Properties of matrix multiplication",
    "sha": "55c15ccc6f71",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "The following properties hold for matrices $A,B,C$ of appropriate dimensions and for scalars $r$.",
      "sha": "5a01d98550b8",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "list",
     "ordered": false,
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The associative law of multiplication",
         "sha": "946d9c080de2",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "(AB)C ~=~ A(BC)."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The existence of multiplicative units",
         "sha": "96dcc7f761d4",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "I_mA ~=~ A ~=~ AI_n,"
       },
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "where $A$ is an $m\\times n$-matrix.",
         "sha": "321a03c14c6b",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "Compatibility with scalar multiplication",
         "sha": "5de3533520ef",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "(rA)B ~=~ r(AB) ~=~ A(rB)."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "The distributive laws of multiplication over addition",
         "sha": "c185a4a605d1",
         "zh": ""
        }
       },
       {
        "k": "math",
        "tex": "\\begin{array}{rcl}A(B+C) &=& AB + AC, \\\\\n      (B+C)A &=& BA + CA.\\end{array}"
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "proof",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "First, we will prove the associative law. In the proof, it will be useful to use *summation notation*. We write",
      "sha": "29be1b9e88da",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i ~=~ x_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "for the sum of the $n$ numbers $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$. Assume $A$ is an $m\\times n$-matrix, $B$ is an $n\\times p$-matrix, and $C$ is a $p\\times q$-matrix. Then both $(AB)C$ and $A(BC)$ are $m\\times q$-matrices. We must show that they have the same entries. The $(i,\\ell)$-entry of the matrix $(AB)C$ is",
      "sha": "6b418cf42c84",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "((AB)C)_{i\\ell} = \\sum_{k=1}^p (AB)_{ik}c_{k\\ell}\n    = \\sum_{k=1}^p (\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}b_{jk})c_{k\\ell}\n    = \\sum_{k=1}^p\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}b_{jk}c_{k\\ell}."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "On the other hand, the $(i,\\ell)$-entry of the matrix $A(BC)$ is",
      "sha": "9761a56076d2",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "(A(BC))_{i\\ell} = \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}(BC)_{j\\ell}\n    = \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} (\\sum_{k=1}^p b_{jk} c_{k\\ell})\n    = \\sum_{j=1}^n\\sum_{k=1}^p a_{ij} b_{jk} c_{k\\ell}."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Both sums are equal, since they are both summing over all the terms where $j=1,\\ldots,n$ and $k=1,\\ldots,p$. Therefore, $(AB)C=A(BC)$. The fact that identity matrices act as multiplicative units was already mentioned in Proposition “Multiplying by the identity matrix”. We leave compatibility with scalar multiplication as an exercise. To prove the first distributive law, assume $A$ is an $m\\times n$-matrix, and $B$ and $C$ are $n\\times p$-matrices. Then both $A(B+C)$ and $AB+AC$ are $m\\times p$-matrices. We have",
      "sha": "62c0e2c9b99f",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "(A(B+C))_{ik} = \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}(B+C)_{jk}\n    = \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}(b_{jk}+c_{jk})\n    = \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}b_{jk} + \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}c_{jk}\n    = (AB+AC)_{ik}."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Thus $A(B+C) =AB+AC$ as claimed. The proof of the other distributive law is similar.",
      "sha": "406e9dda8fe4",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "ex1",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rrr} 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$. Multiply $A$ by each of the following vectors.",
      "sha": "faed7857c927",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "(a)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (b)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (c)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (d)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (e)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\end{array}\\right].\\quad"
    }
   ],
   "sol": [
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "(a)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (b)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (c)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 4 \\\\ -1 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (d)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{array}\\right],\\quad\n    (e)~ \\left[\\begin{array}{r} 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{array}\\right].\\quad"
    }
   ],
