  {
   "id": "ex8",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{array}\\right]$. Find all $2\\times 2$-matrices $B$ such that $AB=0$.",
      "sha": "1841207c6d4f",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "sol": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "$\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{array}\\right] \\left[\\begin{array}{cc} x & y \\\\ z & w \\end{array}\\right] = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc} -x-z & -w-y \\\\ 3x+3z & 3w+3y \\end{array}\\right] = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{array}\\right]$. Solution is: $w=-y$, $x=-z$. So the matrices are of the form $\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} x & y \\\\ -x & -y \\end{array}\\right]$.",
      "sha": "01af4c0f1675",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "source_refs": [
    {
     "relation": "verbatim",
     "site": "Selinger《Linear Algebra: A First Course》（CC BY 4.0 开源教材）",
     "locator": "Properties of matrix multiplication · 习题 3（上游 exercises/Matrices-Multiplication-Multiplication.tex）",
     "url": "https://github.com/selinger/linear-algebra/blob/master/baseText/exercises/Matrices-Multiplication-Multiplication.tex"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ex9",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{array}\\right]$ and $B=\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k \\end{array}\\right]$. Is it possible to find $k$ such that $AB=BA$? If so, what should $k$ equal?",
      "sha": "3c629582c3c1",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "sol": [
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\begin{array}{rcl}\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}\n        1 & 2 \\\\\n        3 & 4\n      \\end{array}\\right] \\left[\\begin{array}{cc}\n        1 & 2 \\\\\n        3 & k\n      \\end{array}\\right] &=& \\left[\\begin{array}{cc}\n        7 & 2k+2 \\\\\n        15 & 4k+6\n      \\end{array}\\right], \\\\\n      \\left[\\begin{array}{cc}\n        1 & 2 \\\\\n        3 & k\n      \\end{array}\\right] \\left[\\begin{array}{cc}\n        1 & 2 \\\\\n        3 & 4\n      \\end{array}\\right] &=& \\left[\\begin{array}{cc}\n        7 & 10 \\\\\n        3k+3 & 4k+6\n      \\end{array}\\right].\\end{array}"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Thus you must have $3k+3=15$ and $2k+2=10$. Solution is: $k=4$.",
      "sha": "9b04be721bee",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
   "source_refs": [
    {
     "relation": "verbatim",
     "site": "Selinger《Linear Algebra: A First Course》（CC BY 4.0 开源教材）",
     "locator": "Properties of matrix multiplication · 习题 4（上游 exercises/Matrices-Multiplication-Multiplication.tex）",
     "url": "https://github.com/selinger/linear-algebra/blob/master/baseText/exercises/Matrices-Multiplication-Multiplication.tex"
    }
   ]
  },
